WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:06.009
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by CuriosityStream, watch over 2,400

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:12.080
documentaries for free for 31 days at curiositystream.com/realengineering.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.920
On December 12th, 1984, the United States
Air Force and NASA began testing an unusual

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:18.920
aircraft.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.080
One that broke all aircraft design convention.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.570
It’s wings pointed forward.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:28.830
However this experimental aircraft dubbed
the X-29, was not the first of its kind.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:33.110
The German’s also experimented with the
concept in the late stages of World War 2

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:38.500
with the Junker JU-287, and it’s prototype
airframes would eventually end up in the hands

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:45.410
of the Soviets who took and developed the
design into the okb-1 ef 131 and OKB-1-140.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:46.410
[1]

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:50.670
All of these early iteration designs ran into
the same problems.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.340
This design was incredibly aerodynamically
unstable.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:59.250
When the wing deflects the force of the oncoming
wind tends to make it deflect even more.[2]

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.440
This is a rather obvious design flaw.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.500
Intuitively you just now looking at it that
something doesn’t quite make sense.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:11.560
So why did Germany, The Soviet Union and the
United States all see the design worthy of

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:12.560
consideration?

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.460
To understand this, we first have to explore
why wings are swept beyond a perfectly perpendicular

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.750
angle in the first place.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.280
Looking at most aircraft developed during
world war 2, you can see that nearly all of

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:25.700
them had straight wings.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.080
The Spitfire, the mitzubishi A6M Zero and
the P-51 Mustang.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.299
It was only during the later stages of the
war, as more powerful engines came to the

00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:39.590
fore that other designs started to emerge,
and in one case the straight wing became a

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:45.280
major design flaw that put the crew of the
P-38 Lightning in serious danger.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.920
These problems arose directly as a consequence
of how wings generate lift.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.340
An aerofoil is designed to make use of bernoulli’s
principle, where a low pressure zone is created

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.880
on top of the wing as a result of airflow
moving faster.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.590
People like to say this false , but it’s
just one of the ways a wing generates lift,

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.260
there is a lot more to the story.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:10.019
Because this airflow actually speeds up as
it crosses the wing, it can reach supersonic

00:02:10.019 --> 00:02:13.659
speeds long before the plane itself reaches
supersonic speeds.

00:02:13.659 --> 00:02:18.799
[3] This causes problems because supersonic
flow means shock waves form, which can disrupt

00:02:18.799 --> 00:02:21.290
normal airflow over the wing.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:27.599
On November 4th 1941, these problems resulted
in the death of Ralph Virden, an expert test

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:31.959
pilot, during a high speed test dive of the
P-38 lightning.

00:02:31.959 --> 00:02:34.629
The causes of the crash were unclear at the
time.

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:39.790
This version of the P-38 had been altered
with superior servos for the control surfaces

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:44.640
to help the pilot overcome aerodynamic stiffening,
where the force of the oncoming air at high

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.959
speeds makes it difficult to move the control
surfaces, but the plane still entered an uncontrolled

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:52.689
dive regardless of these measures.

00:02:52.689 --> 00:02:57.370
The engineers eventually discovered the airflow
was separating from the surface of the wing

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:59.840
as a result of shockwave formation.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:04.279
This reduced the lift the wing could generate,
while increasing the lift on the tail wing

00:03:04.279 --> 00:03:07.349
directly downstream of the flow separation.

00:03:07.349 --> 00:03:11.510
This moved the centre of pressure and forced
the plane to pitch downwards and gain even

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:14.749
more speed,[4] making it next to impossible
to recover from.

00:03:14.749 --> 00:03:19.689
To solve this issue they incorporated a dive
flap on the lower surface of the wing, where

00:03:19.689 --> 00:03:24.151
airflow was not reaching supersonic speeds,
which could be deployed during high speed

00:03:24.151 --> 00:03:28.530
dives to allow the wing to increase lift and
recover from the dive.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:33.599
As technology advanced however aeronautical
engineers started to see that straight wings

00:03:33.599 --> 00:03:39.159
were not suitable for transonic or supersonic
speeds, and gradually started to adopt swept

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:40.159
wings.

00:03:40.159 --> 00:03:44.989
The Germans confirmed the theory with high
speed wind tunnel testing in 1939 by testing

00:03:44.989 --> 00:03:49.969
two wings, a straight wing and a swept wing
with a 45 degree sweep.

00:03:49.969 --> 00:03:54.579
Proving that a swept wing could delay the
onset of supersonic flow AND reduce drag.

00:03:54.579 --> 00:03:59.540
[5] They recognised that the swept wing would
allow a plane to fly faster before shock waves

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:04.769
formed, long before the technology that would
enable them to fly faster was even invented.

00:04:04.769 --> 00:04:11.400
They used this knowledge to develop the Messerschmitt
P.1101, a jet powered plane that could actually

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.979
change it’s sweep angle before flight.

00:04:13.979 --> 00:04:17.780
But the end of the war came before the German’s
could finish it and test the aircraft

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:37.701
Air flow over a wing perpendicular to the
freestream air has one component, the chordwise

00:04:37.701 --> 00:04:42.220
flow, [6] which is air that flows over the
chord of the aerofoil.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:47.400
The chord is the imaginary line running from
the leading edge to the trailing edge of an

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:48.400
aerofoil.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.780
Chordwise flow does accelerate over the aerofoil,
and thus contributes to lowering the speed

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:55.100
at which supersonic flow begins.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.270
Called the critical mach number.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:02.070
Now let’s look at the flow components over
the Bell X-5s wing at its lowest sweep angle,

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:03.480
20 degrees.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.270
Here we can separate the airflow into two
components.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:12.190
[6] The chord wise flow, which is now offset
at a 20 degree angle relative to the freestream,

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:16.840
and the new second component the spanwise
flow, which flows along the length of the

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:21.830
wing and does not accelerate and thus does
not lower the critical mach number.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:27.220
At lower speeds, where supersonic airflow
is not a worry, you want as much of that airflow

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:31.390
to be chordwise and thus generate the necessary
lift to fly.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:35.300
However as the speed of the plane begins to
climb, we are generating more than enough

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:40.670
lift thanks to the increased air speed, and
thus can afford to convert some of that airflow

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:42.230
into spanwise flow.

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:47.140
We do this by increasing the sweep angle,
which the Bell X-5 could do in flight to a

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:49.580
maximum sweep angle of 60 degrees.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:54.710
[7] As the sweep angle increases a larger
portion of that airflow is converted to spanwise

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:59.060
flow, which is great for increasing the top
speed of an aircraft, but can cause some troublesome

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.380
stall characteristics.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:06.100
Because a large volume of air is now originating
at the root of the wing and travelling down

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:11.810
to the tip of the wing, stall will begin at
the tip of the wing and move towards the root.

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:16.270
This is a problem because our ailerons, the
control surfaces that allow us to roll the

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:19.100
plane, are located on the outer wing.

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.230
If stall occurs on the outer wing, we will
lose roll control.

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:27.700
[8] A major problem for say at fighter jet
attempting a high angle of attack maneuver

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:32.630
while maintaining full control, and this is
one of the problems forward swept wings were

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:34.420
trying to fix.

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:39.300
This reverses the direction of the chordwise
flow, so it originates at the wing tips and

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.040
travels to the root of the wing.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:45.690
[9] Meaning stall occurs at the root of the
wing first, allowing us to maintain control

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:47.990
of the plane for much longer.

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:53.670
Not only that, but it reduced induced drag
as a result of wing tip vortices.

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:57.880
Where high pressure air from the lower wing
travels and mixes with low pressure air on

00:06:57.880 --> 00:06:59.810
top of the wing at the wing tips.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:05.990
The Ju 287 was designed this way not to benefit
from the superior aerodynamics characteristics,

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:11.230
but to move the wing box rear wards, which
allowed the bomb bay to move forward closer

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:16.090
to the centre of gravity of the aircraft,
which in turn allowed the plane in-carry a

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:20.400
larger bomb, while not increasing the trim
drag to keep the plane balanced.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:24.810
[10] But ultimately the materials of the time
could not facilitate it.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:29.990
Under normal wing loading, the main force
being exerted on the wing is upwards bending.

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:35.890
Where the force of lift pushes the wing upwards,
while the weight of the fuselage pushes downwards.

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:40.400
To survive this we need to build an adequately
strong and stiff wing.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:45.460
This is achieved through a beam called a spar
which runs the length of the wing.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:50.650
With a forward swept wing an additional stress
is introduced, where the force of oncoming

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:53.170
air is attempting to twist the wing.

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:57.540
We can imagine this with a free body diagram
with springs representing the stiffness we

00:07:57.540 --> 00:07:59.480
need to incorporate into the wing.

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:05.139
[11] Here the kb is the spring stiffness that
will be needed to resist bending, and kt is

00:08:05.139 --> 00:08:08.480
the spring stiffness needed to resist twisting.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:13.510
Creating a structural member that can act
like this torsion spring over the entire wing

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:19.350
however is no easy task and would require
enough additional weight to negate any positive

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:22.150
attributes the forward swept wing would provide.

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:27.160
But that changed when advanced composite materials
became available.

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:32.120
Allowing planes like the X-29 and the Russian
equivalent the SU-47 to be made.

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:37.539
Both planes used carbon fiber reinforced plastics
laid up so the fibres would resist that twisting

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:38.539
motion.

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:43.839
I will focus on the X-29 from here, as information
on American technology is far more freely

00:08:43.839 --> 00:08:45.079
available.

00:08:45.079 --> 00:08:51.139
The X-29s primary structural member for resisting
this twist was a closed box section, located

00:08:51.139 --> 00:08:57.579
here, [12] which was constructed of crisscrossed
composite tape that reached up to 156 layers

00:08:57.579 --> 00:08:58.579
deep.

00:08:58.579 --> 00:09:03.480
Essentially creating that spiral spring shape
within the structural member, but with extremely

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:05.990
stiff and lightweight composites.

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:11.010
Taming that twisting problem, and allowing
the plane to fly successfully.Wind tunnel

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:16.649
tests showed the forward swept design would
provide a 20% gain in efficiency compared

00:09:16.649 --> 00:09:19.100
to same plane with aft swept wings.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:20.100
[9]

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:24.139
This along with a supercritical wing design
[13], which flattens the top edge of the aerofoil,

00:09:24.139 --> 00:09:28.730
to minimise the acceleration of the air over
the top edge, while introducing a concave

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:34.110
curve to the lower surface to increase lift,
allowed the X-29 to spend less fuel flying

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:36.329
at a higher mach number.

00:09:36.329 --> 00:09:41.399
Another drag reducing benefit of the forward
swept wing was the shifting of centre of pressure

00:09:41.399 --> 00:09:46.569
rear wards.[14] Typically the lift generated
by an aft swept wing needs to be counteracted

00:09:46.569 --> 00:09:51.240
by a tail wing which generates downwards pressure
to maintain pitch stability.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:54.649
This downwards pressure is wasted energy that
contributes to drag.

00:09:54.649 --> 00:09:59.990
With a forward swept wing the centre of pressure
is moved to the rear of the aircraft behind

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:05.520
the centre of gravity, and thus to maintain
static pitch control these pitch control surfaces,

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:10.640
called canards, need to generate lift forward
of the centre of gravity and thus contribute

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:12.459
to useful lift.

00:10:12.459 --> 00:10:17.029
This would seem like an obvious feature to
incorporate into every aircraft, but leads

00:10:17.029 --> 00:10:22.580
to instability that requires the control surfaces
to constantly adjust to maintain a stable

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:26.720
flight, and this was one of the massive challenges
the designers faced.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:33.290
The X-29 was incredibly unstable, especially
in pitch, even when compared to modern jet

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:34.339
fighters.

00:10:34.339 --> 00:10:38.709
This means the flight control computers had
to be constantly adjusting the control surfaces

00:10:38.709 --> 00:10:41.990
to maintain stable flight, about 40 times
a second.

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:42.990
[15]

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:48.230
To do this the X-29 had three flight control
computers, to provide redundancy if one failed.

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:52.730
As the plane would become essentially impossible
to fly without the aid of a computer.

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:57.480
Which made it even more worrying when all
three shut down while preparing to take off.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:58.480
[16]

00:10:58.480 --> 00:10:59.779
This caused the plane to be grounded.

00:10:59.779 --> 00:11:05.470
Delayed testing, which was due to accelerate
with the arrival of a second X-29 fitted with

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:10.360
a parachute system to allow high angle of
attack maneuvers to be safely tested.

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:15.850
The spin parachute was installed to provide
positive recovery from spins, as spin-tunnel

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:20.760
tests had indicated that the X-29A ailerons
and rudder provided poor recovery from fully

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:23.480
developed upright spins.

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:27.339
Eventually the problems were solved and high
angle of attack testing resumed and proved

00:11:27.339 --> 00:11:34.110
the X-29s capabilities, but the program ultimately
ended on December 8th 1988, almost four years

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:36.720
to the day of it’s first flight.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:44.430
In between that first and last flight the
X-29 completed 242 flights with 179 combined

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:45.430
flight hours.

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:51.119
Giving valuable scientific data and design
experience in composite airframes and computer

00:11:51.119 --> 00:11:52.869
aided flight.

00:11:52.869 --> 00:11:57.389
Ultimately forwards swept wings weren’t
incorporated into newer generations of planes,

00:11:57.389 --> 00:12:00.320
as the benefits simply did not outweigh the
cons.

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:04.700
From the additional structural requirements,
the poor pitch stability and perhaps most

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:10.589
importantly it’s negative effects on stealth
design [17], forward swept delta wings won

00:12:10.589 --> 00:12:11.769
out in the end.

00:12:11.769 --> 00:12:17.639
This is just one of many unusual plane designs
that originated in world war 2 era Germany,

00:12:17.639 --> 00:12:20.149
among other novel military vehicles.

